{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789420448,"nonce":"GZ6JukImmQjxX7Ak2PaALjRN4Ws=","message":"Stage 4 landed. `import \"thread\" for Thread, Mutex, Lock, Deque`, in\nthe shape of Haxe's sys.thread so a Wren task and an Ash\nfiber-backed thread read the same: Thread.create(fn) places the\nclosure on the worker with the fewest tasks (pending closures count,\nor every task lands on the first idle worker and runs serially —\nthat was the first result); Thread.current, Thread.yield(),\nThread.count. Mutex acquire/tryAcquire/release, Lock wait([ms])/\nrelease (a counting lock), Deque add/push/pop(block)/count. All\nthree keep their state in instance fields: a guard word the natives\nspin on with atomic CAS on the field itself, a count or the item\nlist, a list of waiter tokens — nothing outside the heap, so the\ncollector traces what they hold. A wait parks the task on its\nworld; a release from any thread wakes it through the registry.\n\nWorkers (src/runtime/pool.rs): OS threads with a view and a\nscheduler world each, one per hardware thread on first use, taking\nclosures from their endpoint (roots until taken), running their\nworld and idling safe; the main view's drop shuts them down.\n\nThe corruption the test found: module names were Rc\u003cString\u003e,\ncloned per closure creation and per frame push from every thread —\nnow Arc. e2e threads_share_one_heap_through_a_deque_mutex_and_lock:\n0/100 plain, 0/100 hot, 0/25 stressed, 0/25 hot+stressed after the\nfix (55% before). 8 x fib(35): 0.35 s on one thread, 0.057 s on the\npool on this Mac (10 threads); 0.31 -\u003e 0.061 s on the NUC.\n\nSuites, corpus (tiered, AOT), site and NUC numbers unchanged. One\nthing the hot+stress corpus surfaced is older than this work:\n09e7faa, compiled Url.parse running past a Fiber.abort under GC\nstress, present at 8a0f684.\n\nLeft on this issue: the loop-header poll (a compiled loop with no\nallocation and no tier tick never parks; the tier tick helper is\nthe natural place), a Thread handle from create (Haxe returns one),\nList/Map races are the program's (Go's rule; a spin bit could come\nlater), and hatch-web's Scheduler_ moving onto the runtime scheduler\nor Thread.create — hatch's call.","files":null}]}